/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: IC-203 Securing DevOps Pipelines (DevSecOps) */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["IC-203"] = {
ov:"DevSecOps builds security into the software delivery pipeline rather than bolting it on at the end. This operational course covers the shift-left philosophy, the testing techniques (SAST, SCA, DAST), pipeline and supply-chain integrity, secrets and container security, and the culture that makes it work. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus applied scenarios.",
mods:[
{t:"DevOps and the Shift-Left Philosophy",d:"Why security must move earlier.",
obj:["Explain the shift-left concept","Describe the cost curve of fixing defects late","Explain integrating security throughout the SDLC"],
read:["Traditional security tested software at the end — a gate before release — which meant defects were found late, when they were expensive and disruptive to fix, and often after they had already shipped. Shift-left moves security earlier in the software development lifecycle: security testing and controls run continuously as code is written and built, so defects are caught when they are cheapest to fix and before they reach production. The cost curve is dramatic — a flaw caught in the developer's editor costs a fraction of the same flaw caught in production, in both money and risk.",
"DevSecOps operationalizes shift-left by embedding automated security into the DevOps pipeline that already builds, tests, and deploys software rapidly. Rather than security being a separate team's late gate, security testing (SAST, SCA, DAST — later modules) runs automatically in the pipeline, giving developers fast feedback on the vulnerabilities in their code as they work. The philosophy is that security is everyone's responsibility, integrated into the flow, not a bottleneck at the end. This does not eliminate security expertise — it embeds it into the pipeline and the culture, so that secure defaults and automated checks make the secure path the easy path (the usable-security principle from BC-101)."],
ex:"A developer commits code with a SQL injection flaw. In a shift-left pipeline, the SAST scan flags it within minutes, in the pull request, with a clear explanation — the developer fixes it before it ever merges. In the old model, the same flaw would have passed review, shipped, and been found in a penetration test months later or exploited in production. Catching it at commit time cost minutes; catching it in production would have cost a breach.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-218 (SSDF)","The Secure Software Development Framework."],["DevSecOps foundational literature (DORA/Google)","Shift-left and integrating security into delivery."]],
kc:[
{q:"Shift-left means:",o:["Testing security only at release","Moving security testing and controls earlier in the SDLC, catching defects when they are cheapest to fix","Removing security","Shifting to the left network"],a:1,x:"Shift-left catches flaws early — in the editor or pull request — rather than late in production where they are expensive and risky."},
{q:"The cost of fixing a defect:",o:["Is constant regardless of when found","Rises dramatically the later it is found — a production flaw costs far more than one caught at commit","Is lowest in production","Does not matter"],a:1,x:"The cost curve is steep; catching flaws early in development is far cheaper and safer than fixing them in production."},
{q:"DevSecOps integrates security by:",o:["Adding a final security gate","Embedding automated security testing into the pipeline, giving developers fast feedback as they work","Removing developer responsibility","Slowing delivery"],a:1,x:"Security testing runs automatically in the pipeline, making security part of the flow rather than a late bottleneck."}]},
{t:"The CI/CD Pipeline and Its Attack Surface",d:"The pipeline as critical, targeted infrastructure.",
obj:["Describe the stages of a CI/CD pipeline","Explain why the pipeline is a high-value attack target","Identify pipeline attack vectors"],
read:["A Continuous Integration/Continuous Delivery (CI/CD) pipeline automates building, testing, and deploying software: code is committed, the pipeline builds it, runs tests (including security tests), and deploys it to environments. This automation is powerful and makes the pipeline itself critical infrastructure — it has access to source code, secrets, build systems, and production deployment. Compromising the pipeline is therefore extraordinarily valuable to an attacker: it can inject malicious code into the software (a supply-chain attack affecting everyone who uses it), steal secrets, or deploy to production directly.",
"The pipeline's attack surface is broad: the source code repository, the build system and its plugins, the dependencies pulled in during build, the secrets the pipeline uses, the build agents, and the deployment credentials. High-profile supply-chain compromises (SolarWinds, and others) attacked exactly this — the build pipeline — to insert malicious code that was then signed and distributed as legitimate. This is why the pipeline must be secured as the critical, high-value target it is: hardened build systems, least-privilege pipeline permissions, protected secrets, verified dependencies, and integrity checks (later modules). Treating the pipeline as mere plumbing rather than critical infrastructure is a dangerous blind spot."],
ex:"In the SolarWinds attack, adversaries compromised the build pipeline and injected malicious code into the software during the build process — so the malware was compiled, signed, and distributed as a legitimate update to thousands of customers. The pipeline's access to the build and signing process made it the perfect supply-chain attack vector. Securing the pipeline as critical infrastructure — not plumbing — is the lesson this course drives.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-218 and SLSA framework","Securing the software supply chain and build pipeline."],["CISA, Securing the Software Supply Chain","Pipeline attack vectors and defenses."]],
kc:[
{q:"A CI/CD pipeline is a high-value attack target because it:",o:["Is rarely used","Has access to source code, secrets, and production deployment — compromising it enables supply-chain attacks","Is always offline","Contains no secrets"],a:1,x:"The pipeline's broad access means its compromise can inject malicious code, steal secrets, and deploy to production."},
{q:"The SolarWinds attack compromised:",o:["End-user laptops","The build pipeline, injecting malicious code that was then signed and distributed as legitimate","The firewall","A single database"],a:1,x:"Attacking the build process let malicious code be compiled, signed, and distributed as a trusted update — a supply-chain compromise."},
{q:"The pipeline's attack surface includes:",o:["Only the source code","The repository, build system, dependencies, secrets, build agents, and deployment credentials","Only the deployment step","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Every stage — from source to build to dependencies to deploy credentials — is attack surface that must be secured."}]},
{t:"Secure Source Code Management",d:"Protecting the repository and its integrity.",
obj:["Describe securing source code repositories","Explain branch protection and code review","Explain protecting against repository compromise"],
read:["The source code repository is the starting point of the pipeline and a critical asset — it holds the intellectual property, and changes to it flow through the pipeline to production. Securing it means strong access control (least privilege, MFA, no shared accounts), branch protection (requiring code review and passing checks before code can merge to protected branches), and integrity (signed commits, protected history). Branch protection is especially important: it ensures that no code reaches the main branch — and thus the pipeline — without review and automated checks, making the repository the first shift-left security gate.",
"Code review is both a security control and a quality control: a second set of eyes catches vulnerabilities, backdoors, and mistakes before they merge, and required review (enforced by branch protection) makes it non-optional. Repository compromise is a serious supply-chain risk — an attacker with commit access, or who compromises a maintainer's account, can inject malicious code that flows to production. Defenses include MFA on all repository access (phishing-resistant, per BC-104), monitoring for anomalous repository activity, protecting the CI/CD integration tokens, and requiring signed commits so that unauthorized changes are detectable. The repository is where the software's integrity begins, so its compromise undermines everything downstream."],
ex:"A team enforces branch protection: no code merges to main without a passing SAST scan, an approving review, and signed commits, and all repository access requires phishing-resistant MFA. When an attacker phishes a developer's credentials, the phishing-resistant MFA blocks the login; even had they gotten in, branch protection would require a review to merge malicious code. The repository is secured as the pipeline's first gate, not left as an open door.",
refs:[["GitHub/GitLab security best practices","Branch protection, code review, and signed commits."],["NIST SP 800-218 (SSDF)","Protecting source code integrity."]],
kc:[
{q:"Branch protection ensures that:",o:["Code merges instantly","No code reaches the protected branch (and thus the pipeline) without required review and passing checks","Reviews are optional","Anyone can merge"],a:1,x:"Branch protection makes review and automated checks mandatory before merge — the repository's first shift-left security gate."},
{q:"Code review is valuable as a security control because it:",o:["Slows delivery","Provides a second set of eyes that catches vulnerabilities and backdoors before they merge","Replaces testing","Is only about style"],a:1,x:"Required review catches security flaws and malicious changes before they enter the pipeline — quality and security together."},
{q:"Repository compromise is a supply-chain risk because:",o:["Repositories hold no code","An attacker with commit access or a compromised maintainer account can inject malicious code that flows to production","Code cannot be changed","It only affects one file"],a:1,x:"Malicious commits flow through the pipeline to production; MFA, review, and signed commits defend the repository's integrity."}]},
{t:"Static Application Security Testing",d:"Finding flaws in source code without running it.",
obj:["Explain what SAST does and its strengths","Describe integrating SAST into the pipeline","Explain SAST limitations and false positives"],
read:["Static Application Security Testing (SAST) analyzes source code (or compiled code) without executing it, looking for security flaws — SQL injection, cross-site scripting, insecure cryptography, hardcoded secrets, and other patterns. Its strength is that it examines the code directly and can run early (as code is written, in the IDE or pull request), giving developers fast feedback with the exact file and line of the flaw. This is the archetypal shift-left tool: it catches whole classes of vulnerability at the cheapest possible point, before the code even runs.",
"SAST has characteristic limitations. It produces false positives (flagging code that is not actually exploitable in context) because it reasons about code paths without runtime knowledge, and it can miss issues that only manifest at runtime or depend on configuration (which DAST catches — module six). Effective SAST use means tuning to reduce false positives (so developers trust and act on the findings rather than ignoring them — the alert-fatigue lesson), integrating it into the pipeline as automated checks, and treating it as one layer of a testing strategy rather than a complete solution. A well-tuned SAST that developers trust and act on prevents far more vulnerabilities than an untuned one that floods them with noise they learn to ignore."],
ex:"A developer's pull request triggers SAST, which flags a specific line where user input is concatenated into a SQL query — a clear injection risk — with the file, line, and remediation guidance. The developer fixes it before merge. The tool caught a real, exploitable flaw at the cheapest point. Because the SAST was tuned to minimize false positives, the developer trusted the finding and acted on it rather than dismissing it as noise.",
refs:[["OWASP, SAST guidance","Static analysis strengths, limitations, and integration."],["NIST SP 800-218 (SSDF)","Automated code review in the pipeline."]],
kc:[
{q:"SAST analyzes:",o:["Running applications from outside","Source or compiled code without executing it, finding flaws like injection and hardcoded secrets","Network traffic","Only dependencies"],a:1,x:"SAST examines code statically (without running it), catching whole classes of vulnerability early with file-and-line precision."},
{q:"A key limitation of SAST is that it:",o:["Cannot find any flaws","Produces false positives and can miss runtime/configuration issues, since it reasons without runtime knowledge","Requires running the app","Only works on binaries"],a:1,x:"Static analysis lacks runtime context, causing false positives and missing issues that only manifest at runtime (which DAST catches)."},
{q:"Tuning SAST to reduce false positives matters because:",o:["It speeds the scan","Untuned noise causes developers to distrust and ignore findings — including the real ones","False positives are good","It is not important"],a:1,x:"Alert fatigue applies: a trusted, tuned SAST prevents more vulnerabilities than a noisy one developers learn to ignore."}]},
{t:"Software Composition Analysis",d:"Securing the dependencies you did not write.",
obj:["Explain what SCA does and why it matters","Describe the risk of vulnerable dependencies","Explain SBOM and dependency management"],
read:["Modern software is mostly assembled from third-party and open-source components, so much of an application's security depends on code the developers did not write. Software Composition Analysis (SCA) inventories these dependencies and checks them against vulnerability databases, flagging components with known vulnerabilities (like the Log4Shell vulnerability in the ubiquitous Log4j library, which affected countless applications at once). Because a single vulnerable dependency deep in the dependency tree can expose an application, and because dependencies pull in their own dependencies (transitive dependencies), SCA is essential visibility into risk that SAST of your own code cannot see.",
"SCA connects to the Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) — a formal inventory of the components in a piece of software (detailed in AC-304) — which enables both proactive management (knowing what you use) and reactive response (when a new vulnerability like Log4Shell is disclosed, instantly knowing which applications contain the affected component). Effective dependency management means continuous SCA scanning, keeping dependencies updated, minimizing unnecessary dependencies, and being able to respond fast when a dependency vulnerability is disclosed. The supply-chain risk from dependencies is one of the largest and most exploited attack surfaces in modern software, which is why SCA is a core DevSecOps control."],
ex:"When Log4Shell was disclosed, organizations with SCA and SBOMs instantly identified every application containing the vulnerable Log4j version and patched them fast; those without spent days manually hunting for it while attackers exploited it. The dependency inventory turned a chaotic scramble into a targeted response. SCA's continuous scanning had also been flagging the outdated component before the vulnerability was even public.",
refs:[["OWASP Dependency-Check and SCA guidance","Analyzing dependencies for known vulnerabilities."],["NTIA/CISA SBOM guidance","Software Bill of Materials for dependency visibility."]],
kc:[
{q:"Software Composition Analysis (SCA) checks:",o:["Your own source code","Third-party and open-source dependencies against known-vulnerability databases","Network traffic","Physical security"],a:1,x:"SCA secures the code you did not write — dependencies — flagging components with known vulnerabilities like Log4Shell."},
{q:"An SBOM (Software Bill of Materials) enables:",o:["Faster code","Instantly knowing which applications contain a component when a new vulnerability is disclosed","Encryption","Nothing useful"],a:1,x:"A component inventory turns a new dependency vulnerability disclosure into a targeted response instead of a manual hunt."},
{q:"Transitive dependencies are dangerous because:",o:["They are always safe","Dependencies pull in their own dependencies, so a vulnerable component deep in the tree can expose the application","They cannot be scanned","They are your own code"],a:1,x:"Vulnerabilities can hide in indirect dependencies; SCA provides visibility into the full tree that manual review cannot."}]},
{t:"Dynamic Application Security Testing",d:"Testing the running application.",
obj:["Explain what DAST does and its strengths","Contrast DAST with SAST","Explain integrating DAST into the pipeline"],
read:["Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) tests a running application from the outside, like an attacker — sending inputs, probing endpoints, and observing responses to find vulnerabilities that manifest at runtime. Its strength is exactly SAST's blind spot: it finds issues that depend on runtime behavior, configuration, and the deployed environment — authentication flaws, misconfigurations, injection that only appears when the app runs, and problems in how components interact. Because it tests the actual running application, its findings are real-world exploitable, with fewer false positives about exploitability than SAST.",
"DAST and SAST are complementary, not competing — the recurring layered-testing theme. SAST examines the code early and comprehensively but without runtime context; DAST examines the running app realistically but only exercises the paths it reaches and runs later (requiring a deployed application). A mature pipeline runs both: SAST on every commit for fast code-level feedback, DAST against deployed test environments for runtime validation, plus SCA for dependencies. DAST integrates into the pipeline against a staging environment, and its findings feed back to developers. Interactive testing (IAST) combines aspects of both. The complete testing strategy layers these techniques so that each covers the others' gaps — SAST's code coverage, DAST's runtime realism, SCA's dependency visibility."],
ex:"SAST passed an application's code, but DAST against the running staging deployment finds that an authentication check is missing on an API endpoint — a runtime/configuration flaw invisible to static analysis of the code. The layered approach caught what one technique alone would have missed: SAST cleared the code, DAST caught the deployed reality. Running both, plus SCA, gave complete coverage that any single tool would have left incomplete.",
refs:[["OWASP, DAST and testing guide","Dynamic testing strengths and integration."],["NIST SP 800-218 (SSDF)","Combining static and dynamic testing."]],
kc:[
{q:"DAST tests:",o:["Source code without running it","A running application from the outside, finding runtime and configuration flaws","Only dependencies","Physical hardware"],a:1,x:"DAST exercises the running app like an attacker, catching runtime/config issues that static code analysis cannot see."},
{q:"DAST and SAST are best understood as:",o:["Competing — pick one","Complementary — SAST covers code early, DAST covers runtime realism; a mature pipeline runs both","Identical","Both unnecessary"],a:1,x:"Each covers the other's blind spot; layering SAST, DAST, and SCA gives complete testing coverage."},
{q:"A key advantage of DAST over SAST is:",o:["It runs earlier","Its findings are real-world exploitable, since it tests the actual running application","It sees all code","It needs no application"],a:1,x:"Testing the running app yields realistically exploitable findings with fewer false positives about exploitability."}]},
{t:"Secrets in Code and Pipelines",d:"Keeping credentials out of the codebase.",
obj:["Explain the risk of secrets in code","Describe secret scanning and prevention","Explain pipeline secret management"],
read:["Hardcoded secrets — API keys, passwords, tokens, private keys committed into source code or configuration — are one of the most common and damaging DevSecOps failures (the non-human-identity lesson from BC-104). Once a secret is committed to a repository, it is exposed to everyone with access and, if the repo is ever public or breached, to attackers who scan for exactly this. Worse, secrets persist in git history even after being deleted from the current code, so a naive delete does not remove the exposure — the secret must be rotated. Automated scanners that adversaries run against code hosts find leaked secrets within minutes.",
"Prevention layers several controls. Secret scanning (in the pipeline and as a pre-commit hook) detects secrets before they are committed or catches them in the repository, blocking or alerting. Secrets are kept out of code entirely by using a secrets manager or vault (BC-104) that injects them at runtime, and the pipeline itself uses managed secrets (not hardcoded in pipeline configuration) with least-privilege scope. The modern best practice eliminates long-lived secrets where possible via short-lived, identity-based credentials and workload identity. When a secret does leak, the response is immediate rotation (not just deletion), because the leaked value must be assumed compromised. Secrets management is a core DevSecOps discipline precisely because leaked credentials are such a reliable attack path."],
ex:"A pre-commit secret scanner blocks a developer from committing an API key, and the pipeline's secret scanner would have caught it too. The application gets the key at runtime from a secrets manager instead. When a different key does leak in an old commit, the team immediately rotates it (not just deletes it from history), because the exposed value must be assumed compromised. Layered secret prevention and proper rotation closed the most common leak path.",
refs:[["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","Keeping secrets out of code and managing them properly."],["GitHub/GitLab secret scanning","Detecting and preventing committed secrets."]],
kc:[
{q:"A secret committed to a repository and later deleted from the code:",o:["Is fully removed","Persists in git history and must be rotated, since the exposed value must be assumed compromised","Is automatically safe","Cannot be recovered"],a:1,x:"Deletion does not remove secrets from history; the leaked value must be rotated because it is exposed and likely already scanned."},
{q:"The best way to keep secrets out of code is:",o:["Comment them out","Use a secrets manager that injects them at runtime, ideally with short-lived identity-based credentials","Encode them in base64","Put them in a public file"],a:1,x:"Secrets belong in a vault injected at runtime; eliminating long-lived secrets via workload identity is the strongest approach."},
{q:"Leaked repository secrets are dangerous because:",o:["They are hard to use","Adversaries scan code hosts and find them within minutes, and they often have broad access","Repositories are always private","They cannot be exploited remotely"],a:1,x:"Automated scanning finds leaked secrets fast; secret scanning and proper management prevent this reliable attack path."}]},
{t:"Container Image Security",d:"Securing what you ship.",
obj:["Describe container image vulnerabilities","Explain image scanning and minimal base images","Explain registry and runtime controls"],
read:["Containers package an application with its dependencies into an image, and that image can carry vulnerabilities — in the base image (the OS layer), in the application dependencies (SCA territory), in embedded secrets, or in insecure configuration. Container image scanning (integrated into the pipeline, per BC-103's cloud-native security) checks images for known vulnerabilities and misconfigurations before they are deployed, and rejects images that fail the security bar. Using minimal, hardened base images (distroless or slim images with less installed software) reduces the attack surface — fewer packages means fewer vulnerabilities and less for an attacker to use.",
"Beyond scanning, container security spans the registry (controlling who can push and pull images, so malicious images cannot be introduced) and runtime (running containers with least privilege — non-root, restricted capabilities, read-only filesystems where possible — so a compromised container is contained). The orchestration platform (Kubernetes) adds its own extensive security surface. The DevSecOps pattern is to scan images in the pipeline (shift-left), sign them for integrity (module twelve), control the registry, and enforce least privilege at runtime — so that only scanned, signed, minimal, least-privileged images run. This makes the container a secured unit of deployment rather than a convenient package of accumulated risk."],
ex:"A team scans every container image in the pipeline, rejecting any with critical vulnerabilities or secrets, builds from a distroless base image (minimal attack surface), signs the image, and runs it as non-root with a read-only filesystem. When a base-image vulnerability is later disclosed, the scan flags affected images and the minimal base means fewer such disclosures apply. The container is a secured deployment unit, not a bundle of unexamined risk.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-190","Application container security guide."],["CIS Docker/Kubernetes Benchmarks","Image, registry, and runtime hardening."]],
kc:[
{q:"Container image scanning in the pipeline:",o:["Slows deployment needlessly","Checks images for known vulnerabilities, misconfigurations, and secrets before deployment, rejecting failing images","Only checks the name","Runs after production"],a:1,x:"Shift-left image scanning catches vulnerabilities and secrets before the image ships — a core container control."},
{q:"Minimal (distroless/slim) base images improve security by:",o:["Running faster only","Reducing attack surface — fewer installed packages means fewer vulnerabilities and less for an attacker to use","Adding more tools","Requiring root"],a:1,x:"Less software in the image means fewer vulnerabilities and a smaller attack surface for a compromised container."},
{q:"Running a container as non-root with a read-only filesystem:",o:["Breaks everything","Applies least privilege so a compromised container is contained","Is impossible","Improves nothing"],a:1,x:"Least-privilege runtime configuration limits what a compromised container can do — containment applied to containers."}]},
{t:"Infrastructure as Code Security",d:"Securing the definitions that build environments.",
obj:["Explain Infrastructure as Code and its security implications","Describe scanning IaC for misconfigurations","Explain the leverage of securing IaC"],
read:["Infrastructure as Code (IaC) — Terraform, CloudFormation, and similar — defines infrastructure (networks, servers, permissions, storage) as code that is version-controlled and deployed through the pipeline. This is powerful for consistency and speed, but it means infrastructure misconfigurations (an open security group, a public bucket, an over-permissive IAM policy — the cloud risks from IC-201) now live in code and get deployed automatically and repeatedly. A single insecure IaC template deployed across an environment propagates the misconfiguration everywhere, at machine speed.",
"The leverage cuts both ways, which is why IaC scanning is high-value. Tools like Checkov, tfsec, and OPA scan IaC in the pipeline (shift-left) against security policies, catching misconfigurations before they are deployed — the CSPM concept (IC-201) moved earlier, to the definition rather than the running resource. Catching a public-bucket definition in the pull request prevents the exposure from ever existing, which is far better than detecting it after deployment. Securing IaC also means the same discipline as code: review, version control, and least privilege on the deployment credentials. Because IaC defines the security-relevant configuration of the whole environment, scanning it is one of the highest-leverage DevSecOps controls — fix the template once and every deployment is secure."],
ex:"An IaC scan in the pipeline flags a Terraform template that would create a security group open to 0.0.0.0/0 on a database port and an S3 bucket with public access — before either is deployed. The developer fixes the template, and because the template is reused, every deployment from it is now secure. Catching the misconfiguration in the definition prevented the exposure from ever existing — higher leverage than detecting it post-deployment with CSPM.",
refs:[["Checkov / tfsec / OPA documentation","Scanning Infrastructure as Code for misconfigurations."],["NIST SP 800-218 and cloud IaC guidance","Securing infrastructure definitions in the pipeline."]],
kc:[
{q:"A security risk of Infrastructure as Code is that:",o:["It cannot define infrastructure","Misconfigurations in code get deployed automatically and repeatedly, propagating everywhere at machine speed","It is always secure","It replaces the pipeline"],a:1,x:"An insecure IaC template deploys the same misconfiguration across the environment — leverage that cuts both ways."},
{q:"Scanning IaC in the pipeline is high-leverage because it:",o:["Slows deployment","Catches misconfigurations in the definition before deployment — fix the template once and every deployment is secure","Only checks running resources","Is the same as SAST of app code"],a:1,x:"Catching a misconfiguration in the template prevents the exposure from ever existing — CSPM moved earlier to the definition."},
{q:"IaC scanning relates to CSPM (IC-201) in that it:",o:["Replaces CSPM entirely","Moves the misconfiguration check earlier — to the definition rather than the deployed resource","Is unrelated","Only works in production"],a:1,x:"IaC scanning is shift-left CSPM: catching the misconfiguration in code beats catching it after it is running."}]},
{t:"Policy as Code and Security Gates",d:"Automated guardrails and break-the-build.",
obj:["Explain policy as code and automated guardrails","Describe security gates and break-the-build","Explain balancing enforcement against developer flow"],
read:["Policy as code expresses security and compliance rules as code that the pipeline enforces automatically — Open Policy Agent (OPA) and similar tools let you define policies (no public storage, all images must be scanned, no critical vulnerabilities in production) that are checked programmatically at every deployment. This turns security requirements from documents people are supposed to follow into automated guardrails that are enforced consistently, removing the reliance on manual review and human memory. It is the governance discipline from BC-101 made executable.",
"Security gates enforce these policies at pipeline stages — a gate might block deployment (break the build) if SAST finds a critical flaw, an image scan fails, a secret is detected, or an IaC misconfiguration is present. The break-the-build capability is what gives shift-left teeth: findings that merely warn get ignored, but a gate that blocks deployment forces the issue to be addressed. The design challenge is calibration — gating on genuinely critical issues (so the gate is respected and not bypassed) while not blocking on low-severity noise (which would grind delivery to a halt and breed resentment or bypass culture). Well-designed gates block the things that truly must not ship, provide fast clear feedback, and preserve developer flow for everything else — enforcing security without becoming the bottleneck DevSecOps was meant to eliminate."],
ex:"The pipeline enforces policy as code: a gate breaks the build if SAST finds a critical vulnerability, an image scan fails, or a secret is detected — but merely warns on low-severity findings. When a developer's commit introduces a critical flaw, the build stops with clear feedback until it is fixed; low-severity items are tracked without blocking. The gate has teeth where it matters and preserves flow elsewhere, so it is respected rather than bypassed.",
refs:[["Open Policy Agent (OPA) documentation","Policy as code for automated enforcement."],["NIST SP 800-218 (SSDF)","Automated security gates in the pipeline."]],
kc:[
{q:"Policy as code turns security requirements into:",o:["Documents people should follow","Automated guardrails enforced programmatically at every deployment","Optional suggestions","Manual reviews only"],a:1,x:"Policy as code makes rules executable and consistently enforced, removing reliance on human memory and manual review."},
{q:"The break-the-build capability gives shift-left teeth because:",o:["It is optional","Findings that merely warn get ignored, but a gate that blocks deployment forces the issue to be addressed","It slows everything","It removes security"],a:1,x:"Blocking deployment on critical findings forces remediation, unlike warnings that developers learn to ignore."},
{q:"Well-calibrated security gates:",o:["Block on every finding","Block genuinely critical issues while not gating on low-severity noise, so they are respected not bypassed","Never block anything","Gate randomly"],a:1,x:"Gating on what truly must not ship (while preserving flow elsewhere) keeps gates respected rather than resented or bypassed."}]},
{t:"Supply Chain Integrity: Signing and Provenance",d:"Proving software is what it claims to be.",
obj:["Explain artifact signing and verification","Describe provenance and build integrity (SLSA)","Explain protecting the supply chain end to end"],
read:["A signed artifact can be verified as authentic and unaltered — cryptographic signing of build artifacts (binaries, container images, packages) lets consumers verify that what they run was produced by the legitimate build system and has not been tampered with. This directly counters supply-chain attacks: if the SolarWinds malicious code had required valid signing that the attackers could not forge (rather than being signed by the compromised build system itself), or if consumers had verified provenance, the attack would have been harder. Signing and verification establish trust in the software's origin and integrity.",
"Provenance goes further, attesting to how an artifact was built — from what source, by what pipeline, with what inputs — creating a verifiable record of the software's history. The SLSA framework (Supply-chain Levels for Software Artifacts) defines increasing levels of build integrity, from basic provenance to fully verified, tamper-resistant builds. Protecting the supply chain end to end combines these: secured source (module three), verified dependencies (module five), a hardened pipeline (module two), signed artifacts with provenance, and consumer-side verification. Emerging tools (Sigstore, in-toto) make signing and provenance practical. As supply-chain attacks have become a dominant threat, this integrity chain — proving software is what it claims to be, built how it claims to be — has become a central DevSecOps concern, connecting to the SBOM and SCRM discipline of AC-304."],
ex:"A team signs every container image and generates SLSA provenance attesting it was built from a specific reviewed commit by the hardened pipeline. The deployment environment verifies the signature and provenance before running any image, rejecting anything unsigned or with provenance that does not match. An attacker who somehow produced a malicious image could not forge the signature or valid provenance, so it would be rejected — the integrity chain defeating the supply-chain attack that unsigned deployment would have allowed.",
refs:[["SLSA framework (slsa.dev)","Supply-chain integrity levels and provenance."],["Sigstore and in-toto projects","Practical artifact signing and provenance."]],
kc:[
{q:"Signing a build artifact lets consumers:",o:["Run it faster","Verify it was produced by the legitimate build system and has not been tampered with","Skip testing","Ignore its origin"],a:1,x:"Cryptographic signing establishes authenticity and integrity, countering supply-chain tampering."},
{q:"Provenance (as in the SLSA framework) attests to:",o:["The artifact's price","How an artifact was built — from what source, by what pipeline, with what inputs","Nothing verifiable","The developer's name only"],a:1,x:"Provenance creates a verifiable record of the software's build history, enabling trust in how it was produced."},
{q:"End-to-end supply-chain integrity combines:",o:["Only signing","Secured source, verified dependencies, a hardened pipeline, signed artifacts with provenance, and consumer verification","Only DAST","Nothing"],a:1,x:"The full integrity chain protects every link from source to consumer — proving software is what and how it claims to be."}]},
{t:"DevSecOps Culture and Runtime Feedback",d:"People, metrics, and closing the loop.",
obj:["Explain the cultural shift DevSecOps requires","Describe runtime protection and feedback to development","Identify meaningful DevSecOps metrics"],
read:["DevSecOps is as much cultural as technical: it requires security, development, and operations to collaborate rather than security being an adversarial gate. The cultural shift makes security a shared responsibility, embeds security expertise into teams (security champions), provides developers the tools and training to write secure code, and treats security findings as improvements rather than blame (the no-blame theme). Automated security that gives fast, clear, actionable feedback (not noise) earns developer trust; adversarial, slow, or noisy security breeds resentment and bypass. The culture determines whether the technical controls are embraced or evaded.",
"The loop closes with runtime protection and feedback. Runtime application self-protection, cloud monitoring (IC-201), and production observability detect what the pipeline testing missed, and — crucially — feed that learning back to development so the same class of flaw is prevented next time. This is the DevOps feedback loop applied to security: production informs development continuously. Meaningful DevSecOps metrics measure outcomes and flow (per BC-101): vulnerabilities caught pre-production versus in production, mean time to remediate, security-gate pass rates, and whether security is speeding or slowing delivery. A mature DevSecOps program is measured by secure software delivered fast — security and speed together, not traded off — which is the whole promise of building security in rather than bolting it on."],
ex:"An organization embeds security champions in each dev team, provides fast trustworthy pipeline feedback, and feeds production runtime detections back into development so recurring flaw classes are eliminated at the source. Its metrics show 95% of vulnerabilities caught before production and delivery speed increasing. Security became a shared, embedded practice that sped delivery — the cultural and feedback-loop maturity that makes DevSecOps deliver on its promise.",
refs:[["DORA/Google DevOps research (culture and metrics)","The cultural and measurement dimensions of DevSecOps."],["NIST SP 800-218 (SSDF)","Continuous improvement and feedback in secure development."]],
kc:[
{q:"DevSecOps is as much cultural as technical because:",o:["Culture is irrelevant","It requires security, dev, and ops to collaborate rather than security being an adversarial gate","Only tools matter","Developers write no code"],a:1,x:"The culture of shared responsibility and trusted feedback determines whether the technical controls are embraced or evaded."},
{q:"Runtime feedback closes the DevSecOps loop by:",o:["Ignoring production","Feeding production detections back to development so the same flaw class is prevented next time","Stopping deployment","Removing monitoring"],a:1,x:"Production informs development continuously, applying the DevOps feedback loop to security for continuous improvement."},
{q:"A mature DevSecOps program is measured by:",o:["Number of security tools","Secure software delivered fast — security and speed together, e.g., vulnerabilities caught pre-production and delivery flow","Security slowing delivery","Number of gates"],a:1,x:"The promise is security and speed together; metrics show flaws caught early and delivery not slowed — building security in works."}]},
{t:"Threat Modeling in the SDLC",d:"Designing security in before writing code.",
obj:["Explain threat modeling and its place in the SDLC","Describe a lightweight threat-modeling approach","Explain how threat modeling shifts security even further left"],
read:["The earliest possible shift-left is design: threat modeling analyzes a system's design to identify what could go wrong before any code is written, so security is built into the architecture rather than tested in later. A threat model asks, for a given design: what are we building, what can go wrong, what are we going to do about it, and did we do a good job? Structured approaches like STRIDE (Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege) prompt systematic consideration of threat categories against each component and data flow.",
"Threat modeling is the highest-leverage shift-left because design flaws are the most expensive to fix later — a vulnerability in a single function can be patched, but a fundamentally insecure architecture may require rebuilding. Modeling early identifies missing authentication, unprotected data flows, trust-boundary violations, and single points of failure while they are cheap to address (on a whiteboard). Modern DevSecOps favors lightweight, continuous threat modeling — quick modeling of new features and changes integrated into the development flow — rather than heavyweight one-time exercises. Combined with the automated testing of the later stages, threat modeling ensures security is considered at design, code, build, and runtime — the complete shift-left, from architecture through production."],
ex:"During design of a new payment feature, a lightweight STRIDE threat-modeling session identifies that the data flow between two services lacks authentication (spoofing/tampering risk) and that a failure mode could expose transaction data. Both are fixed in the design — a whiteboard change — before any code is written. Caught at design, they cost minutes; caught in production, the missing authentication would have been a serious breach requiring architectural rework.",
refs:[["OWASP Threat Modeling and STRIDE","Structured threat modeling for the SDLC."],["NIST SP 800-218 (SSDF), design practices","Addressing security in the design phase."]],
kc:[
{q:"Threat modeling is the earliest shift-left because it:",o:["Runs in production","Analyzes the design to find what could go wrong before any code is written","Replaces all testing","Only runs at release"],a:1,x:"Threat modeling builds security into the architecture at design time — the cheapest possible point to address flaws."},
{q:"Design flaws are especially important to catch early because they:",o:["Are always minor","Are the most expensive to fix later — a bad architecture may require rebuilding, unlike a single-function patch","Cannot be fixed","Are caught by SAST"],a:1,x:"Architectural flaws are far costlier than code bugs; threat modeling catches them on the whiteboard where they are cheap."},
{q:"STRIDE is:",o:["A scanning tool","A structured prompt (Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Info disclosure, DoS, Elevation) for systematic threat consideration","A programming language","A compliance framework"],a:1,x:"STRIDE prompts systematic consideration of threat categories against each component and data flow during threat modeling."}]},
{t:"Secure SDLC Frameworks and Compliance",d:"SSDF and demonstrating secure development.",
obj:["Describe the NIST Secure Software Development Framework (SSDF)","Explain demonstrating secure development for compliance","Explain how frameworks tie the practices together"],
read:["The individual DevSecOps practices are organized by secure-SDLC frameworks, chief among them the NIST Secure Software Development Framework (SP 800-218, SSDF). The SSDF groups secure development practices into four categories — Prepare the Organization, Protect the Software, Produce Well-Secured Software, and Respond to Vulnerabilities — providing a common vocabulary and a checklist for whether an organization's software development is actually secure. It ties together everything in this course: source protection, testing, supply-chain integrity, and vulnerability response become named practices within a coherent framework, much as the CSF organized security program outcomes in BC-101.",
"Frameworks matter increasingly for compliance and trust. Government and enterprise customers increasingly require software producers to attest to secure development practices (US executive orders now require SSDF attestation for software sold to the federal government), and demonstrating secure development — with evidence, the way controls are evidenced in BC-106 — becomes a market requirement. This connects DevSecOps to the assurance and supply-chain-risk disciplines: consumers of software want assurance it was built securely, and frameworks like SSDF plus artifacts like SBOMs and provenance (module twelve) provide that assurance. A mature DevSecOps program not only builds secure software but can prove it did, mapping its pipeline practices to the framework and producing the evidence."],
ex:"A software vendor selling to the federal government maps its DevSecOps pipeline to the NIST SSDF practices and produces the required attestation, backed by evidence — SAST/SCA/DAST in the pipeline, signed artifacts with provenance, SBOMs, and a vulnerability response process. The framework turned a collection of practices into a demonstrable, attestable secure-development program, meeting the market and compliance requirement to prove — not just claim — secure development.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-218 (SSDF)","The Secure Software Development Framework practices."],["US EO 14028 and OMB software attestation guidance","Requiring demonstrated secure development."]],
kc:[
{q:"The NIST SSDF (SP 800-218) provides:",o:["A scanning tool","A framework grouping secure development practices into a common vocabulary and checklist","A programming language","A firewall"],a:1,x:"The SSDF organizes secure-development practices (prepare, protect, produce, respond) into a coherent framework, like the CSF for programs."},
{q:"Demonstrating secure development is increasingly a market requirement because:",o:["It is optional","Government and enterprise customers require attestation and evidence that software was built securely","Nobody cares","It slows sales"],a:1,x:"Customers (including the federal government) now require secure-development attestation, so producers must prove — not just claim — it."},
{q:"A mature DevSecOps program can:",o:["Only claim security","Prove it built secure software — mapping pipeline practices to the framework and producing evidence (SBOMs, provenance)","Avoid frameworks","Skip evidence"],a:1,x:"Maturity means demonstrable secure development, backed by evidence, connecting DevSecOps to assurance and supply-chain trust."}]},
{t:"DevSecOps Capstone",d:"Building a secure pipeline end to end.",
obj:["Synthesize the testing, integrity, and cultural practices into a secure pipeline","Sequence the security controls across the SDLC","Judge a pipeline by its ability to prevent insecure deployment"],
read:["This capstone assembles the course into a secure pipeline. Secured source (branch protection, review, MFA, signed commits) feeds the pipeline; on commit, SAST and secret scanning give fast code-level feedback; SCA checks dependencies; IaC scanning checks infrastructure definitions; container images are scanned and signed; DAST validates the running application in staging; policy-as-code gates break the build on critical findings; artifacts are signed with provenance; and runtime monitoring feeds detections back to development. Each control is placed where it is cheapest and most effective (shift-left), and the gates enforce the security bar automatically.",
"The design principle is defense in depth across the SDLC: no single control catches everything, so the layers — SAST for code, SCA for dependencies, DAST for runtime, IaC scanning for infrastructure, secret scanning, image scanning, signing — back each other up, and the pipeline as a whole prevents insecure software from reaching production. A vulnerability SAST misses may be caught by DAST; a dependency flaw invisible to both is caught by SCA; a misconfiguration is caught by IaC scanning; a leaked secret by secret scanning. The pipeline is judged not by any single tool but by whether insecure deployment can get through it.",
"The lesson is that DevSecOps makes secure delivery the default by building security into the pipeline and the culture. The graded deliverable — configuring a CI/CD pipeline that halts deployment if severe vulnerabilities or embedded secrets are found — is exactly this: a pipeline where the secure path is the automatic path, where security gives fast trustworthy feedback, and where insecure software cannot ship without a deliberate, documented exception. An organization that achieves this delivers software both faster and more securely than one relying on a late manual security gate — the core promise of DevSecOps realized.",],
ex:"A team builds a pipeline with every layer, then tests it: a commit with a SQL injection is caught by SAST and blocked; a vulnerable dependency by SCA; a public-bucket IaC template by the IaC scan; an embedded key by secret scanning; a runtime auth flaw by DAST in staging. None reach production, and the feedback is fast and clear. The pipeline is judged sound because insecure software cannot get through it — DevSecOps defense in depth verified end to end.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-218 (SSDF) and SLSA","End-to-end secure software delivery."],["OWASP DevSecOps Guideline","Layering security controls across the pipeline."]],
kc:[
{q:"In a secure pipeline, security controls are placed:",o:["Only at the end","Across the SDLC where each is cheapest and most effective (shift-left), backing each other up","Randomly","Only in production"],a:1,x:"Defense in depth across the SDLC: SAST, SCA, DAST, IaC scanning, secret/image scanning each cover different gaps."},
{q:"A secure pipeline is judged by:",o:["The number of tools","Whether insecure software can get through it — no single tool, but the layered whole prevents insecure deployment","Its speed alone","The vendor"],a:1,x:"The measure is whether the pipeline as a whole prevents insecure deployment, with layers covering each other's blind spots."},
{q:"The core promise of DevSecOps is:",o:["Security or speed, pick one","Secure software delivered faster — security built into the pipeline and culture, not a late manual gate","Slower delivery","Fewer tests"],a:1,x:"Building security in delivers both speed and security; the pipeline makes the secure path the automatic default."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: A SQL injection flaw is caught in the developer's pull request by SAST and fixed before merge. This demonstrates:",o:["Late testing","Shift-left — catching the flaw at the cheapest point before it ever reaches production","A production breach","Wasted effort"],a:1,x:"Catching flaws early in development is far cheaper and safer than finding them in production; that is the shift-left value."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Attackers inject malicious code into a build pipeline; it is signed and shipped to thousands as a legitimate update. This is:",o:["An endpoint attack","A supply-chain attack via the pipeline — like SolarWinds — showing the pipeline is critical infrastructure","A phishing attack","A DoS"],a:1,x:"The pipeline's access to build and signing makes it a supply-chain attack vector; it must be secured as critical infrastructure."},
{q:"SCENARIO: When Log4Shell was disclosed, some organizations identified affected apps instantly and others hunted for days. The difference was:",o:["Luck","SCA and SBOMs — a dependency inventory turns a disclosure into a targeted response","Better firewalls","More developers"],a:1,x:"SCA/SBOM visibility into dependencies enabled instant identification of the vulnerable component across all applications."},
{q:"SCENARIO: SAST passes an app's code, but an API endpoint is missing authentication. What catches this?",o:["SAST again","DAST against the running application — runtime/config flaws are its strength","SCA","Secret scanning"],a:1,x:"Runtime authentication flaws are invisible to static code analysis; DAST tests the running app and catches them — layered testing."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A secret is deleted from the current code after being committed. The correct response is:",o:["Nothing — it is gone","Rotate the secret, since it persists in git history and must be assumed compromised","Recommit it","Make the repo public"],a:1,x:"Deletion does not remove secrets from history; the exposed value must be rotated because it is likely already scanned by adversaries."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An IaC template would deploy a public S3 bucket, but it is caught in the pull request. This is:",o:["Post-deployment CSPM","Shift-left IaC scanning — catching the misconfiguration in the definition before it ever exists","A DAST finding","A runtime alert"],a:1,x:"IaC scanning catches misconfigurations in the template; fixing it once secures every deployment — higher leverage than post-deploy CSPM."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A pipeline gate blocks deployment on critical SAST findings but only warns on low-severity ones. This calibration:",o:["Is too lenient","Keeps the gate respected — blocking what truly must not ship while preserving flow, avoiding bypass culture","Blocks nothing","Is too strict"],a:1,x:"Gating on genuinely critical issues (not noise) keeps gates respected and preserves developer flow — well-calibrated enforcement."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A deployment environment rejects any container image lacking a valid signature and matching provenance. This defends against:",o:["Slow deployment","Supply-chain tampering — an attacker's malicious image cannot forge the signature or valid provenance","Weak passwords","DoS"],a:1,x:"Signing and provenance verification establish artifact integrity, rejecting tampered or illegitimate images."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An organization's security is slow, noisy, and adversarial, and developers bypass it. The root problem is:",o:["Too few tools","Culture — DevSecOps requires collaboration and fast trustworthy feedback, or controls are evaded","Too much automation","Developers are careless"],a:1,x:"The culture determines whether controls are embraced; adversarial, noisy security breeds bypass regardless of the tools."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A pipeline is tested and no insecure code, dependency, secret, or misconfiguration can get through to production. It is judged sound because:",o:["It uses many tools","The layered controls together prevent insecure deployment — no single tool, but the whole pipeline","It is fast","It has one gate"],a:1,x:"A secure pipeline is measured by whether insecure software can pass, with layers (SAST/SCA/DAST/IaC/secret/image) covering each other."}
]};
